---
import SiteLayout from '../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
---

<SiteLayout
  title="About | ArcadeDuck"
  description="The story behind ArcadeDuck: The Simpsons Bowling, DuckStation, Konami GV, audited source provenance, and the projects and people whose work made ArcadeDuck possible."
>
  <section class="page-hero about-hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">About ArcadeDuck</p>
    <h1>This was supposed to fix a bowling game.</h1>
    <p class="page-description">
      ArcadeDuck is an arcade-focused emulator for PlayStation-derived arcade
      hardware. That was not the original plan. The original plan was
      considerably more reasonable: get <em>The Simpsons Bowling</em> working
      properly on a Windows PC.
    </p>
  </section>

  <article class="about-page">
    <section class="about-opening">
      <p>
        Somewhere between that goal and the present day, the project acquired
        SCSI controllers, flash memory, EEPROMs, trackballs, lightguns, arcade
        I/O, board-specific BIOS handling, an arcade-only game database,
        specialized controller profiles, multiple hardware families, and a
        frontend that increasingly wants nothing to do with pretending these
        machines are ordinary PlayStations.
      </p>
      <p class="about-punchline">So that went well.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="about-section">
      <p class="eyebrow">The original problem</p>
      <h2>Why <em>The Simpsons Bowling</em>?</h2>

      <p>
        <em>The Simpsons Bowling</em> has been playable in MAME for years, but
        one of its most noticeable problems has been its audio&mdash;particularly
        the streamed character and announcer voices.
      </p>

      <p>
        And in this particular game, that matters a lot. The cabinet used the
        actual Simpsons voice cast, with characters reacting to throws, talking
        between turns, insulting each other, and generally providing most of
        the personality of the game. The announcer calls strikes, doubles,
        turkeys, and other events throughout play.
      </p>

      <p>
        MAME Testers documents the long-standing issue directly: after the
        first voice/sample is loaded from the CD, later streamed dialogue can
        fail to load. The result is missing character speech and missing calls
        such as &ldquo;Strike,&rdquo; &ldquo;Double,&rdquo; and
        &ldquo;Turkey,&rdquo; with some screens lingering longer while the
        game waits on audio that never plays.
      </p>

      <div class="about-links">
        <a
          href="https://mametesters.org/view.php?id=8017"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noreferrer"
        >MAME Testers issue 08017 &#8599;</a>
        <a
          href="https://adb.arcadeitalia.net/dettaglio_mame.php?game_name=simpbowl&lang=en"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noreferrer"
        >The Simpsons Bowling hardware/game information &#8599;</a>
      </div>

      <div class="about-callout">
        <p>
          When the voices disappear, the bowling still works. Technically.
          But removing most of the dialogue from <em>The Simpsons Bowling</em>
          is a little like watching an episode of <em>The Simpsons</em> with
          half the cast muted. You can probably figure out what is happening,
          but something has clearly gone wrong.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p>
        Then there was the Arcade1Up version. Arcade1Up's Simpsons cabinet used
        a modified version of <strong>DuckStation</strong> for
        <em>The Simpsons Bowling</em>. That implementation demonstrated a
        practical route to running the game with its specialized arcade
        hardware and, importantly, without accepting the missing-dialogue
        experience as normal.
      </p>

      <p class="about-question">
        Can we get that working properly on a normal Windows PC?
      </p>

      <p>That was the beginning of ArcadeDuck. We just didn't know it yet.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="about-section">
      <p class="eyebrow">Milestone one</p>
      <h2>First, make Homer talk.</h2>

      <p>
        The initial goal was not to create another PlayStation emulator.
        DuckStation already existed, and creating a new PS1 emulator from
        scratch because Homer Simpson refused to announce a turkey would have
        been excessive even by our standards.
      </p>

      <p>
        Instead, the goal was to understand the arcade-specific work that had
        already been done around <em>The Simpsons Bowling</em> and make it
        practical on Windows.
      </p>

      <p>
        The game runs on <strong>Konami GV</strong>, a PlayStation-derived
        arcade platform, but its cabinet includes hardware that an ordinary
        consumer PlayStation emulator has absolutely no reason to care about:
        SCSI storage, EEPROM, flash memory, trackball hardware, cabinet inputs,
        arcade-specific boot behavior, and a dedicated daughterboard.
      </p>

      <p>
        In other words, everything required to turn “it's basically a
        PlayStation” into “why the hell isn't this register responding?”
      </p>

      <p>
        The first real milestone was getting all of that working reliably while
        preserving the thing that started the whole project:
        <strong>the voices worked.</strong>
      </p>

      <p class="about-punchline">Mission accomplished. Project over. Obviously.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="about-section">
      <p class="eyebrow">Scope control</p>
      <h2>Then we noticed there were other games.</h2>

      <p>
        Once <em>The Simpsons Bowling</em> was working, something inconvenient
        became apparent: we had not merely fixed one game. We had implemented
        a substantial amount of <strong>Konami GV</strong>.
      </p>

      <p class="about-question">What about the rest of the GV library?</p>

      <p>
        That meant standard joystick games, trackball games, two-player
        trackball games, independent lightguns, specialized control hardware,
        EEPROM and flash persistence, game-specific hardware configurations,
        more accurate SCSI behavior, operator controls, Test and Service modes,
        and all the other things that make an arcade machine an arcade machine
        instead of a PlayStation wearing a very expensive wooden costume.
      </p>

      <p>
        Eventually, supporting the entire Konami GV family became the goal.
        Then GV worked. That would have been an excellent place to stop.
      </p>

      <p>
        What began with <strong>Konami GV</strong> has expanded through{' '}
        <strong>Konami GQ</strong>,{' '}
        <strong>Namco System 11</strong>, and{' '}
        <strong>Sony ZN-1</strong>. Work has continued into later and more
        complicated PlayStation-derived platforms including{' '}
        <strong>ZN-2</strong>,{' '}
        <strong>Taito G-Net</strong>,{' '}
        <strong>Namco System 12</strong>, and additional hardware families.
      </p>

      <p>Because apparently two guns were insufficient.</p>

      <p class="about-punchline">We remain disappointed by this failure of scope control.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="about-section">
      <p class="eyebrow">Audited source provenance</p>
      <h2>Where the current ArcadeDuck source actually comes from.</h2>

      <p>
        ArcadeDuck is built on DuckStation source. The current lineage is not
        inferred from dates, similar files, or old project descriptions. It was
        reconstructed directly from the repository's Git graph during an
        extensive provenance audit.
      </p>

      <div class="lineage" aria-label="ArcadeDuck active source lineage">
        <a
          class="lineage-step"
          href="https://github.com/stenzek/duckstation/commit/2149ab4d6955bfa60cd56856aeb05aebef9e4a1a"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noreferrer"
        >
          <span class="lineage-label">Inherited root</span>
          <strong>DuckStation</strong>
          <span><code>2149ab4d6955bfa60cd56856aeb05aebef9e4a1a</code></span>
        </a>

        <span class="lineage-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&darr;</span>

        <a
          class="lineage-step"
          href="https://github.com/stenzek/duckstation/commit/25bc8a64803df7e702db66e0f11d7b7d0fdc99f2"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noreferrer"
        >
          <span class="lineage-label">Last inherited DuckStation commit</span>
          <strong>GPL-era Git boundary</strong>
          <span><code>25bc8a64803df7e702db66e0f11d7b7d0fdc99f2</code></span>
        </a>

        <span class="lineage-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&darr;</span>

        <div class="lineage-step lineage-current">
          <span class="lineage-label">First ArcadeDuck commit</span>
          <strong>ArcadeDuck active lineage</strong>
          <span><code>53f4ae558af0d569143d7ef7698f1328b71cdec7</code></span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        The first ArcadeDuck commit,
        <code>53f4ae558af0d569143d7ef7698f1328b71cdec7</code>,
        has exactly one parent:
        <code>25bc8a64803df7e702db66e0f11d7b7d0fdc99f2</code>.
        That establishes the active project's inherited DuckStation boundary
        directly from repository history.
      </p>

      <p>
        The following historical DuckStation commit,
        <code>9ca6b5430fb358b39f21ce0b2fc0268de954dd23</code>,
        changed the project's source licensing. The audit therefore treats
        <code>25bc8a6</code> as the Git-graph boundary &mdash; not as a blanket
        claim that every individual file at that revision carried the same
        license.
      </p>

      <div class="about-links">
        <a
          href="https://github.com/stenzek/duckstation/commit/25bc8a64803df7e702db66e0f11d7b7d0fdc99f2"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noreferrer"
        >Inherited DuckStation boundary &#8599;</a>

        <a
          href="https://github.com/stenzek/duckstation/commit/9ca6b5430fb358b39f21ce0b2fc0268de954dd23"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noreferrer"
        >Following licensing-change commit &#8599;</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="about-section">
      <p class="eyebrow">The five-file exception</p>
      <h2>The boundary had five file-level exceptions.</h2>

      <p>
        The repository-level license at the inherited boundary was GPLv3, but
        the audit identified <strong>five source files</strong> that already
        carried <code>PolyForm-Strict-1.0.0</code>-only SPDX headers before the
        broader project licensing change.
      </p>

      <div class="about-code-card">
        <span>PolyForm-only audio rewrite</span>
        <code>00634f68cc0fc187068c9df08a4ea3564938c60a</code>
        <strong>
          src/util/audio_stream.cpp<br />
          src/util/audio_stream.h
        </strong>
      </div>

      <div class="about-code-card">
        <span>PolyForm-only software rasterizer split</span>
        <code>25bc8a64803df7e702db66e0f11d7b7d0fdc99f2</code>
        <strong>
          src/core/gpu_sw_rasterizer.cpp<br />
          src/core/gpu_sw_rasterizer.h<br />
          src/core/gpu_sw_rasterizer.inl
        </strong>
      </div>

      <p>
        That is why ArcadeDuck does not describe <code>25bc8a6</code> as simply
        "the last GPL version." It is the correct Git boundary, but those five
        file-level exceptions required separate treatment.
      </p>

      <p class="about-punchline">
        Apparently even the clean boundary had a boundary condition.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="about-section">
      <p class="eyebrow">Historical cleanup</p>
      <h2>The five exceptions were removed or restored before the first public release.</h2>

      <div class="about-code-card">
        <span>Restoration / removal commit</span>
        <code>3f119ddfc13d51ad68d5134ef09fde54ae23315b</code>
        <strong>August 27, 2026</strong>
      </div>

      <p>
        That commit removed the separate PolyForm-era software rasterizer and
        restored the older software-rendering path. The two audio files were
        substantially restored to the earlier implementation that carried a GPL
        licensing alternative, then continued forward with ArcadeDuck's own
        audio changes.
      </p>

      <p>
        The provenance audit compared the restored source against both the older
        implementation and the intervening PolyForm rewrite. Its normalized
        comparison found{' '}
        <strong>zero retained lines
        unique to the PolyForm rewrite</strong> in the compared current
        audio/backend implementations, while hundreds of meaningful lines
        matched the older implementation.
      </p>

      <p>
        The audited source contained no
        <code>PolyForm-Strict-1.0.0</code>-only or
        <code>CC-BY-NC-ND-4.0</code>-only SPDX declarations in the scanned
        current text headers.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="about-section">
      <p class="eyebrow">Earlier proof of concept</p>
      <h2>Arcade1Up and t-dollaz are part of the history, not the current Git ancestry.</h2>

      <p>
        ArcadeDuck's earliest proof-of-concept work used the{' '}
        <strong>Arcade1Up Simpsons Bowling DuckStation fork</strong> and the
        later <strong>t-dollaz desktop continuation</strong>. Those projects
        were important references for understanding the cabinet-specific
        hardware and getting the original Simpsons Bowling experiment moving.
      </p>

      <p>
        The provenance audit confirmed that the older proof-of-concept is a{' '}
        <strong>separate snapshot history</strong>. It is not an ancestor of
        the current ArcadeDuck <code>dev</code> lineage.
      </p>

      <p>
        They remain credited because they are genuinely part of ArcadeDuck's
        history. They are simply no longer presented as the Git ancestry of the
        source tree being released today.
      </p>

      <div class="about-links">
        <a
          href="https://github.com/Arcade1Up/duckstation-sb"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noreferrer"
        >Arcade1Up / duckstation-sb &#8599;</a>

        <a
          href="https://github.com/t-dollaz/simpsons-bowling-baby-phoenix-duckstation"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noreferrer"
        >t-dollaz continuation &#8599;</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="about-section">
      <p class="eyebrow">Repository-wide review</p>
      <h2>Provenance was checked, not assumed.</h2>

      <p>
        The provenance review enumerated all{' '}
        <strong>2,145 tracked files</strong> in the audited checkout and
        scanned tracked text for SPDX identifiers, copyright notices, license
        text, and upstream/reference markers.
      </p>

      <p>
        Reachable history, branch tips, historical license changes, candidate
        upstream matches, third-party notices, source attribution, and the
        identified file-level licensing exceptions were then reviewed in
        detail.
      </p>

      <p>
        Even minor or possible findings were remediated before the public
        repository was created. The public repository was then started from the
        verified clean source snapshot instead of publishing the old forensic
        development history.
      </p>

      <div class="about-code-card">
        <span>Initial clean public source commit</span>
        <code>13a1d6a02428efe4ec00e531ccd8e29c1fd0bc0e</code>
        <strong>Initial public source release</strong>
      </div>

      <p class="about-punchline">
        Trust is useful. Reproducible commit hashes are considerably more useful.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section class="about-section">
      <p class="eyebrow">Project direction</p>
      <h2>Why not just submit all of this upstream?</h2>

      <p>
        Because this is ultimately a choice about what an emulator should be
        built to do.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Console emulators should emulate console games. Arcade emulators
        should emulate arcade games.</strong>
      </p>

      <p>
        DuckStation is an excellent PlayStation emulator, and ArcadeDuck would
        not exist without the work that came before it. But the farther this
        project developed, the clearer it became that arcade hardware has
        different priorities: coin inputs, Test and Service controls,
        lightguns, trackballs, cabinet I/O, external outputs, EEPROM and flash,
        SCSI devices, board-specific storage, unusual peripherals, dedicated
        sound hardware, networking, and operator behavior that has little or
        nothing to do with emulating a consumer PlayStation.
      </p>

      <p>
        Those features could theoretically be developed as patches against a
        console emulator. That is simply not the direction I want to take.
        ArcadeDuck is intentionally built around the arcade machine first,
        rather than asking a console emulator to accommodate an increasingly
        large arcade subsystem.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>That decision is not about fame. It is not about money. It is not about
        pretending the PlayStation emulation underneath ArcadeDuck was created
        here, and it is certainly not about taking credit away from the people
        whose work made this project possible.</strong>
      </p>

      <p>
        It is a personal development choice: keep the console emulator focused
        on consoles, keep the arcade emulator focused on arcade machines, and
        give each project the freedom to make decisions around its own goals.
      </p>

      <p class="about-punchline">
        Same family tree. Different cabinet.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section class="about-section about-name-section">
      <p class="eyebrow">Brand strategy, allegedly</p>
      <h2>Why “ArcadeDuck”?</h2>

      <div class="name-equation" aria-label="Arcade plus DuckStation equals ArcadeDuck">
        <span>Arcade</span>
        <span class="name-symbol">+</span>
        <span>DuckStation</span>
        <span class="name-symbol">=</span>
        <strong>ArcadeDuck</strong>
      </div>

      <p>That's it.</p>

      <p>
        No advertising agency was consulted. No focus groups were conducted.
        Nobody spent six weeks standing in front of a whiteboard discussing
        brand synergy. No presentation contained the phrase “next-generation
        entertainment ecosystem.”
      </p>

      <p>
        The name simply described what the project was becoming. The
        <strong>Duck</strong> keeps an obvious connection to the DuckStation
        lineage that made ArcadeDuck possible. The <strong>Arcade</strong>
        makes equally clear where the project is going: not another
        general-purpose PlayStation emulator, but an arcade emulator built
        around hardware descended from PlayStation technology.
      </p>

      <p>
        It also provided an entirely unnecessary number of future opportunities
        for duck-related jokes and artwork. That may or may not have influenced
        the decision.
      </p>

      <p class="about-tagline">DuckStation heritage. Arcade cabinet priorities. ArcadeDuck.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="about-section">
      <p class="eyebrow">Hardware research</p>
      <h2>Arcade hardware is weird.</h2>

      <p>
        The farther ArcadeDuck gets from the basic PlayStation architecture,
        the less helpful the phrase “it's basically a PS1” becomes.
      </p>

      <p>
        Yes, many of these machines begin with familiar PlayStation-derived
        components. Then the arcade manufacturer adds a SCSI controller, a hard
        disk, four flash chips, another CPU, a dedicated sound subsystem,
        protection hardware, networking, EEPROM, custom cabinet I/O, a
        trackball daughterboard, three lightguns, or some combination of the
        above.
      </p>

      <p>And suddenly the PlayStation portion is the easy part.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="about-section">
      <p class="eyebrow">Standing on excellent documentation</p>
      <h2>MAME deserves a lot of credit.</h2>

      <p>
        ArcadeDuck exists partly because we wanted a different result for games
        such as <em>The Simpsons Bowling</em>. That should not be interpreted as
        “MAME is bad.” Quite the opposite.
      </p>

      <p>
        MAME is one of the most important technical preservation projects in
        the history of arcade hardware. Its developers and contributors have
        spent decades documenting boards, CPUs, custom chips, ROM layouts,
        storage devices, protection hardware, cabinet I/O, sound systems,
        obscure peripherals, and machines whose surviving manufacturer
        documentation ranges from excellent to “someone uploaded a blurry
        photograph of the PCB in 2004. Good luck.”
      </p>

      <p>
        MAME's hardware research has been an invaluable reference throughout
        ArcadeDuck development. The fact that ArcadeDuck sometimes implements
        a device differently, approaches a problem from another direction, or
        exists because we wanted to improve behavior in a particular game does
        not diminish that contribution.
      </p>

      <p>
        Independent implementations are useful. MAME has broad preservation
        goals across an almost absurd number of machines. ArcadeDuck has the
        luxury of obsessing over a much narrower collection of hardware.
      </p>

      <p class="about-punchline">
        We can spend an unreasonable amount of time on one SCSI timing problem.
        We know this because we have. Repeatedly.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="about-section">
      <p class="eyebrow">More homework</p>
      <h2>Other projects and documentation.</h2>

      <p>
        MAME is not the only technical reference ArcadeDuck uses. MiSTer and
        other FPGA projects can provide valuable independent implementations of
        the same arcade hardware. Original service manuals and schematics are
        used whenever they survive. Chip datasheets are extremely useful. PCB
        photographs can answer questions documentation does not. Other emulator
        implementations can provide clues about undocumented behavior.
      </p>

      <p>
        Sometimes all of those sources agree. Sometimes they don't. Sometimes
        the manual says one thing, another implementation does something else,
        the real game expects a third thing, and you end the evening reading a
        30-year-old NCR SCSI datasheet for entertainment.
      </p>

      <p class="about-punchline">We use the word “entertainment” loosely.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="about-section">
      <p class="eyebrow">Different priorities</p>
      <h2>MAME wasn't wrong. We just wanted something different.</h2>

      <p>
        MAME's mission is enormous. ArcadeDuck's is deliberately narrow. That
        allows ArcadeDuck to make decisions specifically around these machines
        and the way people actually use arcade cabinets.
      </p>

      <ul class="about-feature-list">
        <li>Per-game arcade control profiles</li>
        <li>Lightguns as first-class devices</li>
        <li>Multiple independent trackballs and pointer devices</li>
        <li>Test, Service, Coin, and Start controls built around cabinet use</li>
        <li>Board-specific EEPROM, flash, ROM, CHD, and BIOS behavior</li>
        <li>An arcade game database rather than a retail PlayStation catalog</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        And yes, we can spend a stupid amount of time investigating why Homer
        Simpson isn't talking. Different projects can have different
        priorities. That's a good thing.
      </p>
    </section>

