    </section>

    <section class="about-section">
      <p class="eyebrow">Today</p>
      <h2>What ArcadeDuck is now.</h2>

      <p>
        ArcadeDuck has moved well beyond being a PlayStation emulator with a
        few arcade games attached to it. It is an{' '}
        <strong>arcade emulator focused specifically on hardware derived from
        PlayStation technology</strong>.
      </p>

      <p>
        That means the machine matters as much as the CPU and GPU. ArcadeDuck
        models the things that actually made these systems arcade hardware:
        board-specific BIOS behavior, ROM and CHD layouts, SCSI devices,
        EEPROM and flash storage, cabinet inputs, Test and Service controls,
        lightguns, trackballs, specialized I/O, dedicated sound hardware,
        external outputs, and the strange daughterboards manufacturers added
        because apparently the base hardware was not complicated enough.
      </p>

      <p>
        The interface, game database, control system, storage handling, and
        machine configuration are all being built around arcade hardware rather
        than around the assumptions of a consumer PlayStation.
      </p>

      <p>
        What began with <strong>Konami GV</strong> has expanded through{' '}
        <strong>Konami GQ</strong>, <strong>Namco System 11</strong>, and
        <strong>Sony ZN-1</strong>. Work has continued into later and more
        complicated PlayStation-derived platforms including
        <strong>ZN-2</strong>, <strong>Taito G-Net</strong>,
        <strong>Namco System 12</strong>, and additional hardware families.
      </p>

      <p>
        Some of those later systems remain experimental while their hardware,
        timing, audio, protection, peripherals, and game-specific behavior are
        researched and validated.
      </p>

      <p class="about-question">How far does this eventually go?</p>
      <p>That is still an excellent question. We are still deliberately not asking it.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="about-section about-finale">
      <p class="eyebrow">The short version</p>
      <h2>So why does ArcadeDuck exist?</h2>

      <p>Because <em>The Simpsons Bowling</em> was missing its voices.</p>
      <p>Because getting one game right exposed an entire arcade platform underneath it.</p>
      <p>Because understanding Konami GV made Konami GQ look possible.</p>
      <p>Because System 11 and ZN-1 made stopping there seem unnecessarily sensible.</p>
      <p>Because ZN-2, G-Net, System 12, and several other machines then joined the argument.</p>

      <div class="about-final-question">
        <span>&ldquo;Why doesn't Homer talk?&rdquo;</span>
        <strong>&ldquo;I guess we're building an arcade emulator.&rdquo;</strong>
      </div>

      <p>
        In retrospect, there were probably easier solutions. They would have
        been considerably less interesting.
      </p>
    </section>
